"executive_summary": "This paper presents a comprehensive review of image noise types and denoising algorithms, culminating in a comparative performance analysis. The authors first categorize noise into additive (Gaussian, Salt and Pepper) and multiplicative (Speckle, Brownian) types, detailing their statistical properties and visual manifestations. Subsequently, the paper classifies denoising methods into spatial filtering (linear and non-linear) and transform domain filtering (spatial-frequency and wavelet-based). The wavelet section is particularly detailed, discussing linear filters, non-linear thresholding (hard, soft, semi-soft, Garrote), non-orthogonal transforms (UDWT, SIWPD), and coefficient modeling (deterministic and statistical). The core contribution is a quantitative comparison of six specific denoising filters: Mean Filter (MF), Adaptive Mean Filter (AMF), Pixel-Specific Mean Filter (PSMF), Double-Bit Adaptive (DBA), Modified Double-Bit Adaptive (MDBA), and Modified Double-Bit Universal Threshold Mean Filter (MDBUTMF). These algorithms were tested on a grayscale Lena image with a high noise density of 90%. Performance was evaluated using Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Image Enhancement Factor (IEF). The results indicate that while basic filters like MF and AMF yield low PSNR values (6.5759 and 8.0603 respectively), the advanced adaptive methods (DBA, MDBA, MDBUTMF) significantly outperform them, achieving PSNR values above 17. The MDBUTMF filter demonstrated the highest performance with a PSNR of 17.9865 and IEF of 16.2066. The paper concludes that the selection of a denoising algorithm is application-dependent and requires knowledge of the specific noise type, highlighting promising directions for future research in adaptive and wavelet-based techniques.",
